it's great to be joined today by James  Whitman who's the Ford Foundation  professor of comparative and foreign law  at Yale Law School also author of this  book  Hitler's American model it's so great to  have you on and let's start with the  book many people have some understanding  of what we sort of know colloquially as  Nazi race law but they may not know all  of the different components that were  either inspirations or otherwise sources  for that race law so let's start at the  very beginning Nazi race law refers to  what in the context of World War two  well it's important to distinguish  between the early years of the Nazi  regime and what came later what we  remember most these days is of course  inevitably the Holocaust but in the  early 1930s after Hitler first seized  power the Holocaust was really not yet  on the horizon there was no notion among  the Nazis that they would engage in in  mass killing which seemed if nothing  else in practical the early version of  Nazism that's embodied in the notorious  Nuremberg Laws of 1935 involves a  different program was aim was to force  the Jews to flee from Germany what it  involved in particular was two measures  first the creation of a new form of  second-class citizenship for Jews and  second the introduced introduction of  measures intended to forbid interracial  marriage between Jews and what the Nazis  called re ins that is their concept of  Germans and also extramarital sex and  talk to us a little bit about some of  the some of the specifics of how those  laws were written some of the details  maybe that people have not heard of  observe the logistics of this uh well as  the laws in the sense were written very  hurriedly and reaction to a diplomatic  conflict on with the United States in  September of 1935 but they've been in  preparation for quite a long time and in  particular the preparation involved a  great deal of careful study of American  models that for a very straightforward  reason really the United States was the  leader in innovative racist lawmaking in  the early 1930s something that Europeans  particularly Europeans on the right wing  had been very conscious of for quite a  long time  the Nazis knew it too Hitler himself in  prefer to the United States as the one  state that had made progress for the  creation of a healthy racist order so  let's let's dig into that because for  most people who learned you know us  history history of World War two in  school the sort of general understanding  is hey the United States when when we  got into World War two we were against  Hitler we were against the Nazis there  would be no such connection or  inspiration in terms of anything that  Nazis did at any point from the United  States so talk to us about what  specifically may have been an  inspiration in terms of the Nuremberg  Laws that was going on in the United  States  well it's quite true that when the  United States entered World War two it  was on terms of great hostility with  Nazi Germany there's no doubt about that  but the hostility really didn't clearly  introduce itself into relations between  the two countries until the late 1930s  really not until 1937 or so in the early  1930s the Nazis still saw a great deal  to admire in the United States among  other things they admired Franklin  Roosevelt and his style of government  shocking as it is to hear it now but  beyond that the Nazis saw a great deal  in the American race order to admire  this was of course the era of Jim Crow a  Jim Crow legislation as everybody knows  targeted african-americans in the south  but there was more to it than that there  was a great deal of national and  nationwide race-based legislation in the  United States to the United States was  in particular famous for its immigration  legislation which was designed to  guarantee be continuing dominance of the  white anglo-saxon Protestants who were  thought of as the as the true and  natural inhabitants of the United States  the United States also they created  something of great interest to the Nazis  forms of second-class citizenship not  even so much for african-americans  because their citizenship was at least  in theory guaranteed by the Fourteenth  Amendment but for members of other races  such as Filipinos Puerto Ricans and to  some extent Native Americans this is  something American lawyers had done a  spirit of great creativity but the Nazis  often found admirable how do we know I  mean you talked about this in the book  how do we know that that Nazis were  looking to what was happening in the  United States what what historical  record do we have  well they said so themselves the most  dramatic evidence comes from the  stenographic transcript of a meeting  held in the summer of 1934 to plan the  Nuremberg Laws that meeting a particular  turned on the question of how to  introduce what the United States is  called anti-miscegenation legislation  that is law banning interracial marriage  and the participants in that meeting  engaged in very close examination of  state statutes from all over the United  States in fact at the beginning of the  meeting the Minister of Justice  presented a memorandum on American law  which has been put together by the  Justice Ministry precisely for the  purposes of this discussion beyond that  there was a great deal of published  literature describing not always in  admiring terms but very often with  admiring terms American law among the  published literature may be the most  dramatic example comes from volume  called the National Socialists handbook  for law and legislation from 1934 1935  that described the United States of the  country that had achieved the  fundamental recognition of the need for  race-based law a fundamental recognition  that it was Nazi Germany's calling to  bring to full fruition so the the big  picture of this I'm sure will come sort  of as a shock or surprise to many in our  audience who were not aware of this as I  mostly wasn't before I read your book  generally speaking was there any kind of  effort to suppress knowledge about the  inspiration that not the Nuremberg Laws  drew from what was going on in the  United States or is it has it been  because of lack of interest that it's  not been discussed nearly as much as we  might imagine or why do you think that  this understanding is missing for many  people I don't know if there's been any  deliberate suppression in the United  States and it's not exactly if there has  been in Germany either but the topic is  one that it's very difficult for Germans  to discuss in order to do this work one  has to be willing to plow through an  awful lot of technical German law  and for the most part naturally Germans  who would do that but in Germany these  days it's very very difficult for  serious scholars to make a career if  they're willing to say anything that  would suggest in any way diminishing the  moral responsibility of Germany for the  crimes of the Nazis and in that sense  it's entirely understandable that  Germans have not investigated these  these issues  I suppose you kind of have to be an  American in order to be willing to do it  let's take a pause right there we've  been speaking with James Whitman who's  the Ford Foundation professor of  comparative and foreign law at Yale Law  School this is his book Hitler's  American model we are going to pick it  up with him there on tomorrow's program

